<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zimri-Lim</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zimri-Lim was the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, a city-state on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, in the eighteenth century BCE. His palace archive is a major source for the period. The Elamite ruler was addressed as 'father', denoting superior rank, by kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and Zimri-Lim of Mari.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zimri-lim.jpg" alt="Zimri-Lim" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jolle · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zimrilim.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
